ormation but the whole neighborhood  of pixels means a lot therefore it's not  straightforward to do this whole auto  encoding with transformers on images as  it is on text where the output is more  compressed or well it wasn't  straightforward until this paper  presented an elegant and simple solution  the recipe is  take the image and divide it into  non-overlapping patches this could be  something like 16 times 16 patches so  far we are exactly in the vit setting  and we will stay in the vit setting  where every patch is transformed into  one vector even if this is not  visualized right now in the video but  each patch here is a vector to  understand these prerequisites please  see our previous videos on the  swintransformer vit and dit if you're  not familiar with vision transformers in  general but now instead of taking all  these patch vectors as input it's random  masking time and by masking it's  actually meant removing let's say 75  of the original number of patches are  removed great then the authors take the  vit encoder and encode only these  remaining patches but why did they  remove the patches instead of just  masking them well because the authors  are using a transformer as an encoder  they could have taken all image tokens  including the masked out ones bearing  the value of the mask token but that  would blow up the sequence length this  compute time and memory because then  also masked tokens would need to be  processed in self-attention and then by  the feed forward layer in each  transformer block so because the authors  reduce the sequence length in the image  significantly by just removing 75  percent of the image tokens in the set  they now can use very large encoders by  increasing the parameter number in the  feed-forward networks greatly increasing  the capacity of the encoder but miss  coffeebean they are completely removing  intermediate patches like they never  were there before isn't that confusing  for the encoder no because these things  are almost removed like they were not  there before because the positional  embeddings still remember the fact that  this patch is from the upper part of the  image this other one from the second row  so there are many positions missing  there the the structure of the visual  modality is still encoded in the  positional embeddings so the encoder  with a small sequence length processes  the input and encodes it into the latent  space and the output of the encoder has  the same number of tokens as the input  has because the encoder is a transformer  and that's how usually transformers work  but after training it captures the image  semantics much better okay now it's time  for decoding because we are in an auto  encoder and this is where the mask  tokens are inserted again because now  when reconstructing the original image  the transformer decoder must follow the  transformer architecture one vector per  token in one vector per token out so  from the set of the ideally semantically  structured latent space tokens and mask  tokens the decoder which is also  transformer takes each token and  computes something else which is ideally  the original image patches or almost  because to reconstruct back from vectors  to the output image the authors again  use a linear projection to map the last  activations into the pixel space so that  the output of the model is an image and  the mean squared error can be computed  it's important to note that while the  encoder can be very big in terms of  number of parameters because its  sequence length was significantly  reduced by removing 75 of the tokens the  decoder operates on all patches also on  the mast ones this needs to be  lightweight  and this was the masked transformer  based auto encoder and look the  reconstructions look kind of blurry and  miss coffee bean thinks that one two  papers further this will be working for  high resolution too but you know the  reconstruction quality was not the goal  of this masked auto en